# PowerShell Patterns for mJig

## Script Scope

Parameters are copied to `$script:` variables (PowerShell params are read-only). Nested functions must use `$script:` prefix to modify persistent state.

## LogArray (CRITICAL)

`$LogArray` is a `System.Collections.Generic.List[object]`, NOT a plain array.

```powershell
# CORRECT
$null = $LogArray.Add([PSCustomObject]@{
    logRow = $true
    components = @(
        @{ priority = 1; text = "full"; shortText = "short" }
    )
})

# WRONG — wipes entire log every call
if ($LogArray -isnot [Array]) { $LogArray = @() }
$LogArray += [PSCustomObject]@{ ... }
```

Never use `= @()` or `+=` on `$LogArray`. No guard needed — render loop trims to `$Rows` automatically.

## Collection Allocation (Hot Path)

Pre-allocated collections must be cleared/reused, never recreated:

- `$intervalMouseInputs` (`HashSet[string]`) — use `.Clear()` and `$null = .Add(...)`, never `+=`
- `$script:MenuItemsBounds` (`List[hashtable]`) — use `.Clear()` and `.Add()`
- `$pressedMenuKeys` (hashtable) — use `.Clear()`
- `$_waitPeekBuffer` (`INPUT_RECORD[]`) — reuse as-is

## `[char]` Arithmetic Is Integer Arithmetic (CRITICAL)

`[char] * int` gives an integer, not a string. `[char] + string` throws a type-conversion exception.
Always coerce box chars to `[string]` before any string-building:

```powershell
# CORRECT
$_bh  = "$($script:BoxHorizontal)"   # [string]
$_bv  = "$($script:BoxVertical)"     # [string]
$hline = $_bh * 36                   # string repetition ✓
$row   = $_bv + $content + $_bv      # string concatenation ✓

# WRONG — [char] arithmetic, returns int or throws
$hline = $script:BoxHorizontal * 36          # int!
$row   = $script:BoxVertical + $content      # exception if $content is non-numeric
```

This pattern is required in all dialog files whenever `$hline*` / row variables are built.

## Encoding (CRITICAL)

Box-drawing characters must use `[char]` casts, never literals (prevents encoding corruption):

```powershell
$char = [char]0x250C  # SAFE
$char = "┌"           # RISKY — corrupts if encoding changes
```

## ReadKey Gotcha

Never use `ReadKey("IncludeKeyDown")` alone — it blocks on KeyUp events. Always use:

```powershell
$Host.UI.RawUI.ReadKey("NoEcho,IncludeKeyDown,IncludeKeyUp,AllowCtrlC")
```

Filter KeyDown/KeyUp in code after the read.

## GetDate in Hot Path

`$date = Get-Date` is refreshed each 50ms tick. Use `$date.ToString("HH:mm:ss")` — don't call `(Get-Date)` again.

## Input Registration (CRITICAL — use the helper, never inline)

All user-input flag writes go through `Register-UserInput` (`Private/Helpers/Register-UserInput.ps1`). This is the **only** permitted writer of `$keyboardInputDetected`, `$mouseInputDetected`, `$script:userInputDetected`, `$script:LastUserActivityTime`, and the AutoResume timestamp.

```powershell
# CORRECT — one call sets all flags and timestamps
Register-UserInput -Source Mouse -Date $date

# WRONG — stale pattern, creates inconsistency
$mouseInputDetected = $true
$script:userInputDetected = $true
if ($script:AutoResumeDelaySeconds -gt 0) { $LastUserInputTime = Get-Date }
```

Sources: `Mouse`, `Keyboard`, `Scroll`, `Click`.

## `[string]` Type Annotation Coerces `$null` to `""` — Breaks Color/Object Params (CRITICAL)

Declaring `[string]$Param = $null` causes PowerShell to silently coerce any `$null` passed by a caller to an empty string `""`. Downstream code that checks `$null -ne $Param` or attempts `[ConsoleColor]$Param` will then see `""` instead of `$null`, causing `InvalidCastException` that propagates uncaught through scriptblock invocations and crashes the script.

```powershell
# WRONG — $null passed by caller becomes ""
param([string]$EmojiColor = $null)

# Downstream: $null -ne "" is TRUE → [ConsoleColor]"" → crash
$fgCode = if ($null -ne $FG) { $script:AnsiFG[[ConsoleColor]$FG] } else { 39 }

# CORRECT — untyped; $null stays $null
param($EmojiColor = $null)
```

Use untyped parameters for any optional nullable color, emoji, or object arg. `Write-Buffer` is defensively hardened (`$FG -ne ''` check), but the annotation is the root cause to fix.

## Optional `[ref]` Parameters — Never Annotate With `[ref]` Type (CRITICAL)

Declaring `[ref]$Param = $null` in a `param()` block throws `ParameterBindingArgumentTransformationException` every time the caller omits that argument. PowerShell fails to coerce the default `$null` to the `[ref]` type.

```powershell
# WRONG — crashes when caller omits $MouseDetectedRef
param([ref]$MouseDetectedRef = $null)

# CORRECT — no type annotation on optional ref parameters
param($MouseDetectedRef = $null)
```

Mandatory `[ref]` params (no default) are fine: `[ref]$ManualPauseRef`. Optional ones must drop the annotation. The `$null -ne $Param` guard inside the body is unchanged; callers still pass `([ref]$var)` PSReferences normally.

## LII Classifier (use the helper, never inline)

`Test-UserInputActivity` (`Private/Helpers/Test-UserInputActivity.ps1`) wraps `GetLastInputInfo` + automation filters. Returns `@{ ActivityDetected; MouseMoved }`. Use it in the worker tick, wait-loop, and post-wait path instead of inlining the math.

```powershell
$_liiResult = Test-UserInputActivity -Date $date -CurrentPos $currentCheckPos `
    -LastSimulatedTime $LastSimulatedKeyPress -LastAutomatedTime $workerLastAutomatedMouseMovement
if ($_liiResult.ActivityDetected) { Register-UserInput -Source Mouse -Date $date }
```

## Ring-Buffer Log Append (use the helper, never inline)

`Add-LogEntry` (`Private/Helpers/Add-LogEntry.ps1`) evicts the oldest entry when full and appends a new one. Never call `RemoveAt(0)` + `Add()` inline.

```powershell
# CORRECT
Add-LogEntry -LogArray $LogArray -Rows $Rows -Text "Movement paused" -ShortText "Paused" -Date $date

# WRONG — three-strikes boilerplate; use the helper
if ($LogArray.Count -ge $Rows) { $LogArray.RemoveAt(0) }
$null = $LogArray.Add([PSCustomObject]@{ ... })
```
